.TH CTBREC-MERGE 1 "2026-10-31" "ctbrec-merge" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ctbrec-merge \- losslessly merge and repair ctbrec transport stream recordings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ctbrec-merge
[\fIOPTIONS\fP]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ctbrec-merge
merges MPEG transport stream recordings written by
.BR ctbrec .
When ctbrec is briefly stopped or restarted, a single model's stream is split
into several
.B .ts
parts. This script finds parts belonging to the same model whose gap between
the end of one part and the start of the next is small, and joins them
losslessly (stream copy, no re-encode).
.P
Two layouts are supported:
.IP \(bu 2
One subdirectory per model under
.BR \-\-dir
(the default): each folder's recordings are compared against each other.
.IP \(bu 2
Flat
.B .mts
/.ts
files directly in
.BR \-\-dir ,
in which case
.B \-\-top\-level
is implied and files are grouped by the model name in the filename.
.P
Two filename forms are recognised:
.BI model _YYYY\-MM\-DD_HH\-MM\-SS_mmm.ts
and
.BI model _Chaturbate_YYYYMMDD\-HHMMSS.mts .
.P
By default the join is written by
.B append
mode (see BEHAVIOUR): the earliest part becomes the output and each later part
is copied onto its end with corrected timestamps that place it directly after
the previous part's content, so only the later parts are read and written
instead of the whole output and the merged timeline has no phantom gaps. When a
pre\-flight check fails
(differing transport packet size or PID, an unaligned part, or
.B \-\-keep
or
.BR \-\-no\-append ),
the script falls back to a full
.B ffmpeg \-f concat \-c copy
remux.
.P
.B \-\-fix\-timestamps
switches the script into repair mode. Instead of merging, it scans the
recordings under
.B \-\-dir
and rewrites, in place and losslessly, any whose timeline contains phantom gaps
left by an earlier merge (see BEHAVIOUR).
.P
.SS IO policy
Everything that decides how much of the disk a run may use is controlled by a
single
.B \-\-mode
preset; the individual knobs are still there as overrides but normally do not
need to be touched.
.TP
.B max
Full throughput: no bandwidth cap, no cooldown, no drain waits, and
.B \-\-jobs
defaults to 8 for probing. Use it when nothing is recording.
.TP
.B balanced
(default) Steady 90M/s read and write per device, no burst, a short cooldown
and a drain check before each burst. A merge therefore never saturates the
drive, and a large group no longer ends in a wall of 100% io.
.TP
.B reserved
Leaves the drive to everything else: 40M/s cap, idle ionice class, a long
cooldown, and a strict drain gate. If the disk is still busy after
.BR \-\-io\-wait\-max ,
the run stops instead of adding another burst; the next run picks up where it
left off.
.P
The cap is applied to the ffmpeg doing the work, never to the recorders, so an
unrelated writer keeps its share of the device. In append mode the bytes flow
through this process rather than straight into ffmpeg's own file, so the same
rate is enforced by a token bucket on the copy loop; back\-pressure from the
pipe throttles ffmpeg's read side as well.
.P
The merge is deferred when more than
.B \-\-max\-streams
recordings are open for writing, and the run refuses to start when the
filesystem is below
.B \-\-min\-free\-pct
percent free.
Every mergeable group is processed unless
.B \-\-max\-run\-gb
sets an output budget (unlimited by default).
.P
A file that is currently open for writing is never merged. The script scans
.B /proc
for open write file descriptors and matches them to recordings by inode
(spare device and inode number) rather than by path, so recorders running in a
container, where the same files appear under a different path, are still
detected. Files modified within
.B \-\-age
seconds are additionally skipped as a fallback. Parts whose video (codec,
dimensions) or audio (codec, sample rate, channels) parameters differ are left
alone. The nominal frame rate is not compared: variable\-framerate sources
report an unstable value from a header\-only probe, which would otherwise block
otherwise\-identical parts.
.P
Requires
.BR python3 (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1),
and
.BR ffprobe (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-mode \fI\,max|balanced|reserved\/\fR
Choose the io policy preset. Defaults to
.BR balanced .
Each preset fills in every pacing knob below; any knob given explicitly on the
command line still wins.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Print the measured disk busy percentage, io stall percentage, unflushed
(\fIDirty\fR + \fIWriteback\fR) megabytes, the detected device, whether the
bandwidth cap can be applied, and the thresholds of the active mode, then exit
without merging. Useful for answering "is the disk busy right now and why".
.TP
.B \-\-dir \fIDIR\fR
Base directory to scan. Defaults to
.BR /mnt/pve/Data_Storage/ctbrec .
.TP
.B \-\-gap \fISECONDS\fR
Maximum gap, in seconds, between the end of one part and the start of the next
for the parts to be merged. Defaults to
.BR 3600
(60 minutes).
.TP
.B \-\-slack \fISECONDS\fR
Extra slack, in seconds, allowed when pre\-selecting candidates by modification
time. Defaults to
.BR 30 .
.TP
.B \-\-age \fISECONDS\fR
Skip files modified within the last
.I SECONDS
seconds, as a fallback active\-file check. Defaults to
.BR 300 .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-lsof
Do not run the open\-for\-write activity scan; rely on
.B \-\-age
only, and skip the
.B \-\-max\-streams
deferral.
.TP
.B \-\-keep
Move the original parts to a
.B .merged_originals/
subdirectory instead of deleting them. This disables append mode (the first
part must stay in place to be extended), so a full concat remux is used.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the merge plan, including the chosen mode, without changing anything.
The
.B \-\-max\-streams
deferral is ignored in this mode, so the plan can be previewed while many
recordings are still being written.
.TP
.B \-\-top\-level
Only process files directly in
.BR \-\-dir ;
never enter subdirectories.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Check the joined output before deleting the originals (extra full read of the
output). The duration is compared against the expected timeline, and the raw
transport PTS is scanned for discontinuities. In append mode the expected
timeline is
.B one part after another
with the wall\-clock gaps removed.
.TP
.B \-j, \-\-jobs \fIN\fR
Number of parallel header probes. Defaults to 1, except 8 under
.BR "\-\-mode max" ;
probing is I/O\-light and serial probing avoids seek thrash on a busy disk.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-append
Disable append mode and always remux via
.BR "ffmpeg \-f concat" .
.TP
.B \-\-min\-free\-pct \fIPERCENT\fR
Refuse to start when the filesystem holding
.B \-\-dir
is less than
.I PERCENT
free. Defaults to
.BR 3.81 ,
which is roughly 75 GB on the 2 TB recording volume.
A non\-zero exit is returned.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-streams \fIN\fR
Defer the whole run when more than
.I N
recordings are open for writing. Defaults to
.BR 4 .
Ignored under
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
.TP
.B \-\-cooldown \fISECONDS\fR
Minimum time to sleep before starting another burst. Defaults to 5 under
.BR balanced ,
30 under
.BR reserved ,
and 0 under
.BR max .
.TP
.B \-\-io\-wait\-max \fISECONDS\fR
Wait up to this long, after
.BR \-\-cooldown ,
for the disk to drain before starting a burst. Defaults to 120 under
.BR balanced ,
600 under
.BR reserved ,
and 0 (no gate at all) under
.BR max .
.TP
.B \-\-io\-wait\-threshold \fIPERCENT\fR
Io stall percentage considered drained, measured from the cumulative
.B full
counter in
.B /proc/pressure/io
over the sample window rather than from
.BR avg10 .
The cumulative form is deliberate:
.B avg10
is a decaying average and reads 0 the moment a burst pauses, even while
hundreds of megabytes of dirty pages are still owed to the disk. Defaults to 10
under
.BR balanced ,
3 under
.BR reserved .
.TP
.B \-\-disk\-busy\-threshold \fIPERCENT\fR
Device busy percentage (from
.BR /proc/diskstats )
considered drained. Defaults to 40 under
.BR balanced ,
15 under
.BR reserved ,
100 (ignored) under
.BR max .
.TP
.B \-\-drain\-dirty\-mb \fIMB\fR
Megabytes of
.B Dirty
plus
.B Writeback
still owed to the disk that counts as busy. This is the signal a burst has
finished at the filesystem level even though no process is blocked yet: the
write went into the page cache and the stall only appears later, in a kworker.
Defaults to 256 under
.BR balanced ,
64 under
.BR reserved ,
0 (ignored) under
.BR max .
.TP
.B \-\-drain\-sample \fISECONDS\fR
Length of each measurement window when checking for drain. Defaults to
.BR 1 .
.TP
.B \-\-drain\-timeout\-action \fI\,proceed|stop\/\fR
What to do when the disk is still busy after
.BR \-\-io\-wait\-max .
.B proceed
starts the burst anyway;
.B stop
ends the run with the remaining groups left for the next one. Defaults to
.B proceed
under
.B max
and
.BR balanced ,
and to
.B stop
under
.BR reserved .
.TP
.B \-\-max\-run\-gb \fIGB\fR
Stop after roughly
.I GB
of merged output in one run.
.B 0
(the default) disables the budget so a single run processes every group.
.TP
.B \-\-fix\-timestamps
Repair mode: scan the recordings and remux, in place, any whose container
duration far exceeds its actual content, removing the phantom timeline gaps of
an earlier merge. No merging is performed in this mode.
.TP
.B \-\-fix\-threshold \fIRATIO\fR
Repair files whose container\-duration / content\-duration ratio exceeds
.IR RATIO .
The ratio is about
.B 1.0
for a file with no phantom gaps. Defaults to
.BR 1.05 .
.TP
.B \-\-fix\-sample \fISECONDS\fR
Seconds of packets sampled from the start of a file when estimating its content
bitrate and so its content duration. Defaults to
.BR 60 .
.TP
.B \-\-nice \fIN\fR
Nice level for the merge and repair ffmpeg. Defaults to 0 under
.BR max ,
10 under
.BR balanced ,
19 under
.BR reserved .
.TP
.B \-\-io\-class \fI\,best-effort|idle\/\fR
Ionice class for the merge and repair ffmpeg. Defaults to
.B best\-effort
(nice 7) under
.B max
and
.BR balanced ,
and to
.B idle
under
.BR reserved .
.TP
.B \-\-io\-rate \fIRATE\fR
Hard per\-device read and write bandwidth cap for the ffmpeg doing the merge or
repair, applied with
.BR systemd\-run (1)
via
.B IOReadBandwidthMax
and
.BR IOWriteBandwidthMax .
Defaults to
.B 90M
under
.BR balanced ,
.B 40M
under
.BR reserved ,
and
.B 0
(no cap) under
.BR max .
.B 0
leaves only
.BR nice " and " ionice .
The cap is skipped when no block device backs
.B \-\-dir
or when a systemd scope cannot be created; the script warns once and falls back
rather than failing.
.TP
.B \-\-io\-weight \fIN\fR
cgroup io weight (1\-10000) for the scope hosting that ffmpeg. Defaults to 1000
under
.BR max ,
100 under
.BR balanced ,
10 under
.BR reserved .
.TP
.B \-\-io\-device \fIDEV\fR
Block device for
.BR \-\-io\-rate
and for the busy\-time measurements.
Defaults to the device backing
.BR \-\-dir ,
detected with
.BR findmnt (1).
When
.B \-\-dir
is on a network filesystem there is no block device and the cap is skipped.
.SH BEHAVIOUR
.IP \(bu 2
Each candidate part is probed with a short, header\-only
.B ffprobe
read for start time, codec parameters, and stream PIDs. Duration is estimated
from the filename start time and the file modification time, avoiding a full
read of every candidate.
.IP \(bu 2
Adjacent parts with matching parameters, matching PIDs, and a gap within
.B \-\-gap
are joined.
.IP \(bu 2
Append mode copies each later part with
.BR "ffmpeg \-c copy \-output_ts_offset" ,
placing it directly after the previous part's content (subtracting the part's
own start timestamp) with a small margin so timestamps stay monotonic and the
merged timeline stays continuous. The result is written straight onto the end
of the earliest part. Before extending, the first part's original length is
recorded; if ffmpeg fails or a size or verify check fails, the file is
truncated back and the originals are kept. Only the appended parts are removed
on success.
.IP \(bu 2
In repair mode each candidate is measured by comparing its container duration
with the duration implied by its size and its opening\-seconds bitrate. Files
above
.B \-\-fix\-threshold
are remuxed with
.BR "ffmpeg \-c copy"
to a
.B .part
file, the raw transport PTS is scanned for discontinuities, and the file is
replaced only when the timeline is continuous; a failed repair never destroys
the original.
.IP \(bu 2
Concat fallback (and
.BR \-\-keep )
writes an
.BI name .part
file which is renamed into place only after it is complete, so a half\-written
file never looks final.
.IP \(bu 2
Before each group, free space is checked against the bytes that group will add
(plus a margin); a group that would not fit is skipped.
.IP \(bu 2
Progress is printed before scanning each model and before each ffmpeg append
or remux, so a long SMR garbage\-collection stall is not mistaken for a hang.
.IP \(bu 2
A burst is paced on both sides. While it runs, ffmpeg is executed inside a
systemd scope carrying the
.B \-\-io\-rate
cap and the mode's io weight; in append mode the copy loop additionally
sleeps to hold the same average rate, which also throttles ffmpeg's input
through pipe back\-pressure. There is therefore no such thing as "a large merge
that pins the disk at 100%": the group simply takes longer.
.IP \(bu 2
Before a burst, and after any group that actually wrote output, the run
sleeps
.B \-\-cooldown
seconds and then polls until the disk is quiet for two consecutive samples or
.B \-\-io\-wait\-max
expires. "Quiet" means device busy below
.BR \-\-disk\-busy\-threshold ,
io stall below
.BR \-\-io\-wait\-threshold ,
and unflushed data below
.BR \-\-drain\-dirty\-mb ;
all three must agree. When it never gets quiet the mode decides between
starting anyway and stopping the run. Groups that write nothing are not paced,
and
.B \-\-dry\-run
never waits.
.IP \(bu 2
Every merged file reports its own throughput (elapsed time and MB/s), so a slow
group is visible in the log instead of looking like a hang.
.IP \(bu 2
The output keeps the first part's filename, so the merged file stays in the
same directory as its inputs.
.IP \(bu 2
Originals are deleted only after the output size is within tolerance of the
expected size (and, with
.BR \-\-verify ,
the duration matches). If ffmpeg fails or a check fails, the originals are
kept.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B .part
Temporary output used by the concat fallback and by repair mode.
.TP
.B .merged_originals/
Where originals are moved when
.B \-\-keep
is used.
.SH EXAMPLES
Preview the merge plan for the default server directory:
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-merge \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.P
Merge with the default balanced policy (90M/s cap, short waits):
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-merge
.EE
.P
Use the whole disk because nothing is being recorded:
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-merge \-\-mode max
.EE
.P
Stay out of the way of a live encode on the same spindle:
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-merge \-\-mode reserved
.EE
.P
Ask what the disk is doing right now:
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-merge \-\-status
.EE
.P
Merge with the originals kept (forces a concat remux):
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-merge \-\-keep \-\-verify
.EE
.P
Ignore the activity gate and all drain waits:
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-merge \-\-mode max \-\-max\-streams 999
.EE
.P
Merge the old flat directory layout:
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-merge \-\-dir /mnt/shared/Ctbrec/Recording \-\-top\-level
.EE
.P
Preview and then repair merged files that have phantom timeline gaps:
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-merge \-\-fix\-timestamps \-\-dry\-run
ctbrec-merge \-\-fix\-timestamps
.EE
.P
Repair without a bandwidth cap (only
.B nice
and
.BR ionice ):
.IP
.EX
ctbrec-merge \-\-fix\-timestamps \-\-io\-rate 0
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Completed normally, or deferred because too many recordings were active.
.TP
.B 1
.B \-\-dir
is not a directory, or the filesystem is below
.BR \-\-min\-free\-pct .
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted; a partial
.B .part
output is removed and an in\-progress append is rolled back.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ctbrec-merge-remote (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR ffprobe (1),
.BR systemd-run (1),
.BR findmnt (1)
